  /* Below this breakpoint the frame gives up the letterbox-free,
     never-crop treatment above and instead fills most of the screen with
     object-fit:cover, cropping whatever overflows. That's a deliberate
     breakpoint-scoped exception to "never crop" — the alternative
     (sizing the frame to the photo's own aspect ratio) left too little
     room to keep the identity block overlaid on the photo without it
     spilling into the banner above and the section below.
     Height is tied to viewport width (min() with an svh cap) rather than
     pinned to a flat 100svh, so it actually gets shorter as the screen
     narrows instead of walling off the same full-height box at every
     mobile size — 130vw keeps enough room for the identity content
     without needing the full screen, and the 100svh half of the min()
     still protects short/landscape phones from a frame taller than the
     viewport itself. The strong vignette above stays as-is — it's still
     load-bearing for text contrast now that text is back on the photo at
     every breakpoint. */
  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .carousel-frame {
      max-width: none;
      aspect-ratio: auto;
      height: min(100svh, 130vw);
    }

    .carousel :global(.carousel-slide) {
      object-fit: cover;
    }

